Let the apparent depth of P be $\mathrm{x}_1$ and the apparent depth of the image of P be $\mathrm{x}_2$.
$\therefore \quad \mathrm{x}_1=\frac{\mathrm{d}-\mathrm{h}}{\mu}$ and $\mathrm{x}_2=\frac{\mathrm{d}+\mathrm{h}}{\mu}\therefore \quad$ Apparent distance between P and its image is
$\begin{aligned}
\dot{x}_2-x_1 & =\frac{d+h}{\mu}-\frac{(d-h)}{\mu} \\
& =\frac{d+h-d+h}{\mu}=\frac{2 h}{\mu}
\end{aligned}$

$As optical paths are equal,
\begin{aligned}
& \mu_{\mathrm{g}} \mathrm{x}_{\mathrm{g}}=\mu_{\mathrm{m}} \mathrm{x}_{\mathrm{m}} \\
& \mathrm{x}_{\mathrm{m}}=\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{g}} \mathrm{x}_{\mathrm{g}}}{\mu_{\mathrm{m}}}=\frac{1.6 \times 3}{1.25}=3.84 \mathrm{ cm}
\end{aligned}$

